  "summary": "Sailors etched ships, sweethearts and everything into whale teeth with jackknives and soot.",
  "description": "Scrimshaw is the sailor-made folk art of the Yankee whaling fleet: whale ivory (teeth, jaw panbone) and walrus ivory incised with jackknife and sail needle, the lines blacked with lampblack, soot or India ink. Whalers spent idle months between catches making: portraits of sweethearts, full-rigged ships, whaling scenes, whales themselves, liberty poles, game boards, and ornate pierced-and-turnery swifts and canes for home. The classics — Frederick Myrick's signed \"Susan's Teeth\" (1829), the whale-ship \"New Bedford\" panbones — anchor every maritime museum, and the craft's images are the visual memory-book of American whaling life.",
  "culturalContext": "Scrimshaw is Yankee whaling's memory-book: sailor-made teeth and swifts recorded ships, sweethearts and the industry's violence, and the New Bedford collections now frame it within whaling's environmental reckoning — folk art as both maritime heritage and testimony of the industry that nearly emptied the oceans.",
